      "seed": 1690695207,
      "version": 6,
      "versionNonce": 2047762202,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1794797333590,
      "created": 1794794897957,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "customData": {
        "slidesOrder": 9,
        "mcpSlideLayout": "chart",
        "mcpSlideDesign": {
          "style": "excalidraw",
          "accent": "violet",
          "decor": true
        },
        "presenterNotes": "- Controlled admission simulation: 120 independent requests for distinct EAs arrive together, and each job lasts exactly one simulated second.\n- The before schedule is derived from upstream immediate-execution code. The after schedule interleaves production SQLite submit and claim paths during arrival, with simulated completions.\n- The queue admits at most 100 concurrent jobs. Initially 20 wait; all 120 complete across two simulated seconds. Bursts up to 100 independent jobs fit in one wave when capacity is free.\n- Each EA still permits only one active execution. Distinct initiating requests preserve the normal twenty-submission limit per request.\n- This is an admission illustration, not a real-model latency or throughput benchmark. The asynchronous scheduler, per-EA contention, overflow, and restart recovery are outside this fixture and covered separately by tests.\n- Source: docs/charts/measurements.json and docs/charts/README.md."
      },
      "name": "10 — EA execution concurrency"
    },
